+{"body":"## What is wanted\n\nOne map of the automated tasks in the delivery lifecycle, from ideation to a merged pull request and on into post-merge work. For each task it states:\n\n- whether the skill behind it runs as its own run or as a step in an existing run;\n- which component it belongs to: tracker, git host, runner, orchestrator or observability;\n- its trigger, inputs and outputs, so tasks stay modular and can be composed.\n\n## How the map is built\n\nThrough interviews with the owner, run with `/planning:wayfind`: one decision at a time, each answered by the owner. Repository data (transcripts, the plugin inventory, run records) is research input to those interviews. It does not settle a decision on its own, and a provisional answer drawn from it does not stand in for the owner's.\n\n## Why\n\nSkills and lanes have grown one at a time. Nothing shows the whole sequence, so gaps, overlaps and work that could share a run are hard to see, and each new lane adds load to a shared GitHub API budget.\n\n## First item on the map\n\nA simplify pass on every pull request, so that it no longer has to be done locally. Placement is open: a step inside the pull request, or a post-merge run with its own pull request. Choose whichever is most efficient. It must never slow the pull request.\n\n## Research inputs\n\n- Skill use recorded in session transcripts.\n- The full plugin inventory, placed across the lifecycle.\n- Work done per agent and per Actions run.\n\n## Open question\n\nDoes this need a UI over the lifecycle, or are the map and the existing reports enough? The map should supply the evidence for that decision.\n\n## Deferred items for the map\n\n### A merge starts the next run\n\n**What it is:** a merge on the git host starts the next run, so the work adapts its pace to how fast the developer wants to move that day.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** it is a strong accelerator, so it stays open. Merges here are already unattended, so a merge-started run could start further agent work with no person in the loop. The map must decide its brakes before any task starts this way.\n\n### Vendor-agnostic providers\n\n**What it is:** the direction for how the factory is built:\n\n- Vendor-agnostic and dependency-inverted, with providers you bring plus built-in implementations.\n- Public repositories per concern: runner, orchestrator and observability.\n- Tracker and git host are wired-up providers and are never rebuilt.\n- One shared system, rather than separate ones for work and personal use.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** this is direction without a design yet. The map is where it gets one.\n\n### Runner choice per task\n\n**What it is:** each task may run on the runner class that fits it, for example a cheap hosted CI runner for review and an isolated VM for implementation. Today one runner class serves every lane.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** it changes the shape of the automation, and the map decides which tasks exist and where each runs before any task gets its own runner class.\n\n### A factory spec handed to an agent\n\n**What it is:** a written spec of the whole factory, complete enough that an agent can build it and run it.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** the map is the input such a spec would describe, so the spec follows the map.\n\n### A factory plugin\n\n**What it is:** a plugin that ships skills to build, maintain, curate, update and extend a factory, for any downstream repository, this one included. This repository holds the beginnings of one.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** which skills it ships follows from the tasks on the map.\n\n### Two runner designs for building a spec\n\n**What it is:** two ways to build a set of dependent tickets unattended:\n\n- one runner per frontier, whose subagents build the unblocked tickets in parallel on one machine and one checkout, with no merge step;\n- one runner per ticket, each isolated on its own machine, with a merger runner that combines their branches.\n\nThe first shares one environment and needs no merge; the second keeps each ticket's failure and changes apart at the cost of a merge step and a runner per ticket. Neither is preferred yet.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** the choice depends on where the map places implementation and review, and on the runner question above.\n\n### Vendor research for runners, storage and hosting\n\n**What it is:** research of the runner, storage and hosting vendors a factory could use, what each does, and current alternatives to each.\n\n**Why it is deferred:** public repositories stay on Actions and nothing is being bought now. The research belongs to the infrastructure design this map leads into, run as a `/planning:wayfind` effort.\n\n## Decision items from the workshop 08 alignment pass (2026-11-24)\n\nThe owner sent these here to be decided with him, each as its own item. Run the first two first.\n\n1. **Simplicity audit.** Run `/overengineering:audit` and compare our workflows with the course's four (43 workflows and 57,947 lines against 1,727), looking for duplication and over-engineering. Its findings become the next items. No line-count ceiling until then.\n2. **One knob block per lane.** Every lane gets the same three settings (on/off, open-item cap, cadence), checked by one shared script before any model runs. Triggers are filtered so a run that would only skip never starts: `intake-reenter` ran 486 times on 2026-11-24 and skipped 468, and about 12,600 runs on 2026-11-22 were mostly skipped fan-out.\n3. **Lane renames by responsibility,** starting with `pr-refine`, which only runs `fix-docs`. Each rename touches 58 to 240 files.\n4. **Split lanes with more than one reason to change:** intake (verdict, admission, refinement, size split), work-unblock (unblock, status sync, re-fire), and pr-unblock (conflicts, feedback, dependency review, CI fixes).\n5. **Terms with several meanings:** \"triage\" names four things and \"refine\" three; `status: confirmed` means verified in `/work-items:triage` and parked in the pipeline.\n6. **Issue state** as an org field or as labels. The owner's model: a `needs-\u003cthing\u003e` label as the command, an in-progress state per activity, and a past-tense label as the event. Event-driven, with no polling; chains may fork and join.\n7. **The dossier branch's** role across the lifecycle.\n8. **A \"needs a prototype\" intake exit,** as the course does, before the spec.\n9. **Rename `docs/conventions/pr-pipeline.yaml`** to match its real scope. It configures every lane, not only the PR pipeline.\n\nBuilt outside the map, each as a session-owned issue: #18859 (intake on the skill-activity path), #18860 (one lane id list and stage vocabulary), #18861 (collaborative canvas mode), #18862 (the architecture scan), #18863 (lane output format), #18858 (a guardrails bug).\n\n## Done when\n\nThe map is committed, each task on it has the fields above, the UI question has a recorded answer, and every deferred item has a recorded outcome.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n","labels":[],"number":17133,"title":"feat(lanes): map every automated task from ideation to merged pull request","user":{"login":"kyle-sexton"}}
